<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; background-color: #e0f2f7; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What is Artificial Intelligence? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-style: italic; margin-top: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Intelligent Robots</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What is Artificial Intelligence?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Give a simple, prepared presentation on current events or past experiences. Interactive: Participate in debates on issues of general interest, current or historical.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can give a simple presentation about the future impact of artificial intelligence by expressing hypothetical outcomes (e.g., "If AI continues to evolve, it might change the job market significantly.")<br>Can participate in debates, responding to arguments and agreeing or disagreeing with respect and clarity (e.g., "It is agreed that AI could create jobs, but new skills may also be required.")<br>Can conduct interviews about AI and technology, asking open questions (e.g., "How do you think Panama's logistics and trade industries could change with AI integration in the future?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of different types of robots or AI applications (e.g., a robot in a factory, a self-driving car, a smart home assistant). Students are asked to call out what they see and share a brief opinion. The teacher asks, "What do you think is the most significant impact intelligent robots will have on society in the future?" Students share initial thoughts, focusing on words like 'job,' 'adopt,' 'start,' 'robot' to implicitly practice the /ɑː/ sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express predictions using conditional forms and future simple, incorporating target vocabulary. For example, "If AI continues to advance, it will revolutionize how we work," or "AI systems are programmed to learn, and they might improve customer service." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of the long vowel sound /ɑː/ in words like "robot," "job," "adopt," "start," and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words such as "innovative" /ˈɪn.ə.veɪ.tɪv/ and "technological" /ˌtek.nəˈlɒdʒ.ɪ.kəl/, encouraging choral and individual repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'If AI improves, our lives will change,' am I talking about the past or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'robots are programmed,' who is doing the programming?" (Expected answer: Someone or something is programming the robots, not the robots themselves.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio interview excerpt (approx. 1-2 minutes) between a journalist and an AI expert discussing the future impact of artificial intelligence on job markets and society. The excerpt will feature predictions, hypothetical situations, and descriptions of AI functionality using target grammar (conditional forms, future simple, passive voice) and vocabulary (e.g., 'opportunities,' 'challenges,' 'employment,' 'algorithms,' 'evolve,' 'revolutionize'). The register is formal but accessible, reflecting a news report or podcast format.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing personal opinions, predictions, and hypothetical situations about the future of technology and its societal role.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the main topic of the interview?" and "Does the expert see AI's future as mostly positive or negative?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The expert believes that AI will drastically reduce all types of employment." (False) "According to the expert, AI systems are developed to enhance human capabilities." (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing conditional forms (If... will/might), Future Simple (will/won't), and Passive Voice (is/are programmed, will be enhanced) while using vocabulary related to AI's impact (e.g., 'job markets,' 'opportunities,' 'challenges,' 'advance,' 'evolve,' 'integrate,' 'society,' 'technological').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Conditional Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students work individually to complete sentences with appropriate verbs and clauses, focusing on conditional forms. E.g., "If AI ______ (advance), it ______ (create) new employment opportunities." (will create). "Many tasks ______ (be automated) if AI technology ______ (integrate) further." (will be automated, integrates). The teacher circulates, checking for accuracy in grammar and pronunciation, especially for /ɑː/ sounds in words like "job" or "start."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. AI Impact Chain (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill):</b> In small groups, students take turns making a prediction about AI's future impact. The first student starts with a sentence using a conditional or future simple. E.g., "If AI systems are improved, customer service will be enhanced." The next student must build on that idea, using another conditional or future simple sentence and new vocabulary. E.g., "If customer service is enhanced, societal experiences will shift drastically." Teacher provides prompt cards with target vocabulary (e.g., 'algorithms,' 'functionality,' 'revolutionize,' 'adapt,' 'significant,' 'technological').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Predict the Shift (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two topic cards (e.g., "AI in Healthcare," "AI in Education," "AI in Logistics"). For each topic, they must discuss and predict at least three hypothetical outcomes and their potential challenges or opportunities, using conditional forms, future simple, and passive voice. They take turns asking and answering questions like "How do you think AI might change jobs in Panama's logistics and trade industries in the future?" and "What challenges will be created if AI is fully integrated into education?" They check each other's use of grammar and try to use varied vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "AI Impact Debate"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will participate in a structured debate on a controversial statement related to the future impact of Artificial Intelligence and intelligent robots. They will be divided into two teams: "Pro-AI" and "Anti-AI" (or "Cautious-AI").</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Statement for Debate:</b> "Artificial Intelligence will create more opportunities than challenges for human society in the next decade."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Preparation (5 min):</b> Teams briefly prepare their arguments, discussing hypothetical outcomes, opportunities, and challenges. They must incorporate conditional forms (e.g., "If AI continues to evolve, it might change the job market significantly."), future simple (e.g., "AI will create new employment opportunities."), passive voice (e.g., "New guidelines must be established."), and target vocabulary (e.g., 'society,' 'job markets,' 'employment,' 'functionality,' 'responsible,' 'drastic,' 'innovative').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Debate (10-12 min):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each team presents opening arguments (2 min per team).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Rebuttal/Response rounds (2 min per team, alternating). Students must listen and respond to arguments, agreeing or disagreeing with respect and clarity (e.g., "It is agreed that AI could create jobs, but new skills may also be required.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Closing statements (1 min per team).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher's Role:</b> Facilitate the debate, ensure fair turns, encourage respectful interaction, and observe language use. Do not interrupt for corrections unless communication breaks down.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' participation in the "AI Impact Debate."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Appropriate and accurate use of conditional forms, future simple, and passive voice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Integration of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words (e.g., 'opportunities,' 'challenges,' 'evolve,' 'revolutionize,' 'societal,' 'job markets') naturally within their arguments.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Arguments are clear, well-structured, and logically connected.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Overall intelligibility, with attention to the /ɑː/ sound in words like "job," "robot," and clarity in multisyllabic words (e.g., "innovative," "technological").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Skills:</b> Ability to respond to arguments, agree/disagree respectfully, and maintain a conversational flow.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I shared about AI's impact today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One area where I want to improve my speaking about complex topics like AI is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reflective practice for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I use conditional forms and future predictions to express my ideas during the debate?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific vocabulary related to AI did I find most useful, and which words do I need to practice more for better fluency?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well during the speaking activities, especially in promoting interaction and the use of target grammar and vocabulary? (e.g., "The debate format genuinely engaged students, and they spontaneously used many of the target conditionals.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further improve their speaking accuracy and fluency regarding complex topics like AI? (e.g., "Some students struggled to form complex sentences with the passive voice under pressure; a quick review or more sentence-building exercises might be needed. Also, further explicit pronunciation practice of multisyllabic words would be beneficial.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking practice and the ideas generated during the debate will directly prepare students for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), where they will construct written arguments about AI's future impact, incorporating the same grammatical structures and vocabulary in a more formal, structured manner.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>